<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pata Pata</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first recorded 1956 or 1959; best-known recording <a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Pata Pata&quot; means &quot;touch touch&quot; in Xhosa, the language in which the song was first written and sung. It was also the name of a dance popular in the shebeens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>'s townships in the mid-1950s, in which partners patted each other in time to the music. Makeba's song was not the only one the dance inspired.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the dance, the man crouched before his partner and patted her body in time to the music as he rose and she spun with circling hips; in another version the men stood in a row with arms held out while the women patted each in turn, after which the men did the same. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miriam-makeba">Miriam Makeba</a>'s melody was based on an instrumental, Phatha Phatha, by Shumi Ntutu and Isaac Nkosi, which in turn drew on Noma Kumnyama by Alson Mkhize. Dorothy Masuka's Ei Yow Phata Phata of 1956 was a distinctly different song, though Masuka later recorded Makeba's version and claimed to have written it herself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Makeba first recorded the song in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> with her girl group, the Skylarks. In 1967, after building a career in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, she recorded it again with Jerry Ragovoy as producer, adding a spoken part in English, and Ragovoy was billed as co-writer of the words and music. The English lines describe the dance done down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> way, and a spoken passage says that every Friday and Saturday night is Pata Pata time, the dance going on until the morning sun begins to shine. The record appeared on her album of the same name and as a single, with Malayisha on the flip side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The single reached number 12 on the Billboard Hot 100 on 25 November 1967, and the song is regarded by many as Makeba's signature hit. She returned to it often: a duet with Chayanne in 1988, a new recording on her album Welela in 1990, and Pata Pata 2000 on her album Homeland. On the night she died, she performed it just before she collapsed on stage.</p><p>Many others have recorded it, from Lynn Taitt's rocksteady instrumental of 1966 and versions by Tito Puente, the Supremes and Sylvie Vartan to later ones by Osibisa, Manu Dibango, Thalía, the Skatalites, Angélique Kidjo and Pink Martini. It has been heard in films, television series and advertisements, among them a Honda commercial of 2009 and the second season of The White Lotus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pata Pata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
